#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# macos-defaults — apply the macOS settings that are worth scripting.
#
# Deliberately run by hand rather than automatically on `chezmoi apply`, because
# it changes the feel of the machine and you should be looking at it when it
# does. Everything else lives in System Settings; see SETUP.md for that list and
# for why the split falls where it does.
#
# Every value here is one that is genuinely non-default, so this reproduces a
# specific machine rather than encoding somebody's opinion. Verified on macOS
# 15.2 (24C101).
#
# Two things to know about how `defaults` works, because they explain most
# "this command did nothing" confusion:
#
#   1. `-g` is shorthand for NSGlobalDomain, the preference domain every
#      application reads. Other lines target an application's own domain.
#   2. Applications read their domain once at launch and cache it in memory.
#      Writing the file on disk does not reach a running process. Restarting
#      Dock is enough for the Dock settings; the NSGlobalDomain ones need a
#      logout and login.

set -euo pipefail

if [[ "$(uname -s)" != "Darwin" ]]; then
  echo "macos-defaults: not macOS, nothing to do." >&2
  exit 0
fi

echo "Applying macOS defaults..."

# === Keyboard ===
# Both values are measured in 15-millisecond frames, not milliseconds.
# KeyRepeat 1 repeats every ~15ms; InitialKeyRepeat 10 waits ~150ms before
# repeating starts. System Settings' fastest positions are 2 and 15, which is
# the entire reason these are written directly.
defaults write -g KeyRepeat -int 1
defaults write -g InitialKeyRepeat -int 10

# The setting that makes the two above matter, and the one with no checkbox
# anywhere in System Settings. `true` (macOS's default) makes holding a key open
# the accent picker — hold `e`, get è é ê ë. `false` makes it repeat instead.
# Leaving this at the default is why fast key repeat feels inconsistent between
# applications.
defaults write -g ApplePressAndHoldEnabled -bool false

# === Dock ===
defaults write com.apple.dock launchanim -bool false        # no GUI toggle exists
defaults write com.apple.dock magnification -bool true
defaults write com.apple.dock largesize -int 52             # GUI is a slider you cannot land on a number
defaults write com.apple.dock mru-spaces -bool false        # stop macOS reordering Spaces, so Space 3 stays Space 3

# Disable the bottom-right hot corner. Recent macOS defaults it to Quick Note,
# which fires whenever the pointer parks in that corner. 1 means disabled.
# Hot corners live in the Dock's domain, so the killall below applies them.
defaults write com.apple.dock wvous-br-corner -int 1
defaults write com.apple.dock wvous-br-modifier -int 0

# === Windows and sound ===
defaults write -g AppleMiniaturizeOnDoubleClick -bool false # double-clicking a title bar should not minimise
defaults write -g com.apple.sound.beep.volume -float 0      # mute the system alert beep

# Note on what is NOT here: trackpad settings. On macOS 13 and later they are
# held by daemons that cache them and rewrite the plist when System Settings
# opens, so scripting them produces changes that silently revert. Set those in
# System Settings — in particular, leave secondary click on "two fingers" and
# not "click bottom right corner".

killall Dock

cat <<'EOF'

Done. The Dock has been restarted, so its settings are live.

The NSGlobalDomain settings — key repeat, press-and-hold, double-click, alert
volume — are read by each application when it launches. Log out and back in for
them to take effect everywhere.

Verify afterwards with:
  defaults read -g KeyRepeat              # expect 1
  defaults read -g ApplePressAndHoldEnabled  # expect 0
EOF
